<article class="prose">
    <div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="help">Help</a> / <a href="help/articles">Articles</a></div>
    <h1>How to read your pay stub</h1>
    <p class="prose-sub">Where your money goes between "gross" and what lands in your account.</p>

    <GuideShot Image="employee-paystub.jpg" Caption="A sample WageHarbor pay stub." />

    <h2>The top: who, when and which period</h2>
    <p>Your employer's name and address, your name, employee number and the last four digits of your SSN. The <strong>pay date</strong> is when you're paid; the <strong>period</strong> is the dates of work this paycheck covers.</p>

    <h2>Earnings</h2>
    <p>What you earned before anything is taken out: your salary or hours × rate, plus any overtime, holiday, paid time off, bonus or commission. The total is your <strong>gross pay</strong>.</p>

    <h2>Taxes</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Federal income tax</strong>: based on your earnings and the choices on your Form W-4.</li>
        <li><strong>Social Security</strong> (6.2%) and <strong>Medicare</strong> (1.45%): together called FICA. Your employer pays a matching share on top of your pay.</li>
        <li><strong>State income tax</strong>: in states that have one, based on your state withholding form. Some states also have other employee taxes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Deductions</h2>
    <p>Money taken out for benefits or other reasons, such as health insurance, retirement (401(k)), HSA or a garnishment. Deductions marked <strong>pre-tax</strong> are taken out before some taxes are worked out, which lowers your taxable wages.</p>

    <h2>Employer-paid benefits</h2>
    <p>What your employer pays toward benefits for you, such as part of your health insurance. It isn't taken out of your pay; it's shown so you can see the full value of your benefits.</p>

    <h2>Net pay and where it went</h2>
    <p><strong>Net pay</strong> is gross pay minus taxes and deductions: what you actually receive. If you have direct deposit, the stub shows how it was split between your accounts.</p>

    <h2>Year-to-date (YTD)</h2>
    <p>Running totals for the calendar year. Your W-2 at year end will match the YTD figures on your last pay stub of the year (allowing for some pre-tax items).</p>

    <h2>Something looks wrong?</h2>
    <p>Check your hours, rate and W-4 first. If it still doesn't add up, ask your HR or payroll person. They can correct it on a future paycheck. With WageHarbor, your pay stubs are always under <strong>My pay</strong>, and you can print or save any of them as a PDF. See the <a href="help/employee">employee guide</a>.</p>

    <p class="prose-note">This article is general information, not tax advice. Tax rates shown are the standard federal employee rates and can change.</p>
</article>
